{"from": "text:mishnah-sukkah-1-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-07-02-feast-of-booths", "grade": "B", "note": "Sukkah 1:1 regulates the sukka ('a sukka... more than twenty cubits high is unfit') - booth-dwelling as a regulated festival practice, the same institution John 7:2 names. Compiled c. 200 CE and may not describe 30s CE practice exactly."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-sukkah-4-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-07-02-feast-of-booths", "grade": "B", "note": "Sukkah 4:5: 'Each day they would circle the altar one time and say: Lord, please save us' - the Temple liturgy of the Feast of Booths. Compiled c. 200 CE and may not describe 30s CE practice exactly."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-shabbat-19-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-07-22-sabbath-circumcision", "grade": "B", "note": "Shabbat 19:5: 'When the eighth day of a baby's life occurs on Shabbat, he must be circumcised on that day' - the same Sabbath-circumcision rule John 7:22-23 presupposes. Redacted c. 200 CE and may not describe 30s CE practice exactly."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-312", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-07-35-diaspora", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus (Ant. 18): diaspora Jews deposited 'that half shekel which every one, by the custom of our country, offers unto God' in treasury cities, 'whence, at a proper time, they were transmitted to Jerusalem' - diaspora communities institutionally connected to Jerusalem in the first century. It attests the Dispersion's existence and ties, not the crowd's quip."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-sukkah-4-9-water-libation", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-07-37-water-libation", "grade": "B", "note": "Sukkah 4:9: 'One would fill a golden jug with a capacity of three log with water from the Siloam pool' - the water-libation rite of the Feast of Booths, the rite against which the 'rivers of living water' saying is spoken. It attests the rite, never the saying. Compiled c. 200 CE and may not describe 30s CE practice exactly."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-5-siloam", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-07-37-water-libation", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus (War 5.140-145, c. 75 CE): Jerusalem's wall 'extended as far as Siloam; for that is the name of a fountain which hath sweet water in it, and this in great plenty also' - the named Siloam water source in the right period, the setting of the libation. It attests the water source, not the Gospel saying."}
{"from": "site:pool-of-siloam", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-07-37-water-libation", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated Second Temple Pool of Siloam (Bliss and Dickie 1894-97; Shukron and Reich from 2004) confirms the pool as a standing water installation at Siloam in the Second Temple period - the source the libation rite presupposes. Reused from Luke 13. It attests the setting, not the saying."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-6-288-300", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-07-32-officers-sent", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus (War 6.5.3): the temple watchmen ran 'to the captain of the temple' - the Temple maintained an organized guard under an officer who could be dispatched; the institution behind 'the chief priests and Pharisees sent officers'. Josephus writes of the temple as it stood before 70 CE; it attests the institution, not this arrest order."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-sanhedrin-4-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-07-51-hearing-before-judgment", "grade": "B", "note": "Sanhedrin 4:1 regulates capital procedure ('the court judges during the daytime, and concludes the deliberations and issues the ruling only in the daytime') - a formal process of examination and deliberation before condemnation. The mishnah does not state Nicodemus's personal-hearing maxim verbatim; compiled c. 200 CE and may not describe 30s CE practice exactly. Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-07-14-taught-in-temple-mid-feast", "grade": "B", "note": "The Second Temple stood in Jerusalem through the 30s CE as the city's place of teaching and gathering (reused node); it attests the setting of the teaching, not what was taught."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-sukkah-4-9-water-libation", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:sefaria-sukkah-4-9"}
{"from": "text:2-kings-14-25", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:bible-api-2-kings-14-25"}
{"from": "text:2-kings-14-25", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-jonah"}
{"from": "claim:jn-07-22-sabbath-circumcision", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:leviticus-12-3", "note": "Leviticus 12:3: 'In the eighth day the flesh of his foreskin shall be circumcised' - the biblical command behind the custom; a cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:jn-07-22-sabbath-circumcision", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:genesis-17-12", "note": "Genesis 17:12 fixes circumcision at eight days old; a cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:jn-07-52-no-prophet-from-galilee", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:2-kings-14-25", "note": "2 Kings 14:25 names the prophet Jonah 'of Gath-hepher' (in the northern kingdom, lower Galilee) - the biblical counter-example the objectors overlooked; a cross-reference, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "claim:jn-07-41-christ-from-galilee", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:2-samuel-7", "note": "2 Samuel 7:12-16, the Davidic promise behind the expectation that the Christ comes 'of the offspring of David'; a cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:jn-07-41-christ-from-galilee", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-02-05-micah-bethlehem", "note": "Difference of context, not of fact: Matthew 2:5-6 has the chief priests cite Micah for Bethlehem as the Christ's birthplace; John 7:41-42 has Jerusalemites object that the Christ was not expected from Galilee. The same Scripture-based expectation, applied differently."}
{"from": "claim:jn-07-14-taught-in-temple-mid-feast", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-26-55-taught-in-temple", "note": "Same practice (Jesus teaching in the Temple), different occasion: John 7:14 at the middle of the Feast of Booths; Matthew 26:55 during the final week."}
{"from": "claim:jn-07-05-brothers-unbelieving", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-12-46-jesus-brother-james", "note": "Matthew 12:46-47 has the mother and brothers seek Jesus (standing outside); John 7:3-5 adds that during the ministry the brothers urged him to go publicly to Judea and did not believe in him - a detail only John gives."}
{"from": "passage:jn-07-03", "type": "related_to", "to": "passage:mt-12-46", "note": "Different events with the same cast: Matthew 12:46 has his mother and brothers standing outside seeking to speak to him; John 7:3-5 has the brothers urging him to go publicly to Judea before Tabernacles."}
{"from": "passage:jn-07-05", "type": "related_to", "to": "passage:mk-03-21", "note": "Different events with similar content: Mark 3:21 has those close to him go out to seize him, saying 'He is insane'; John 7:5 states the brothers' unbelief directly, in the Tabernacles context."}
{"from": "passage:jn-07-42", "type": "related_to", "to": "passage:mt-02-06", "note": "Different events with similar content: Matthew 2:6 quotes Micah about Bethlehem in the birth narrative; John 7:42 has the festival crowd cite 'the Scripture' in the messianic debate - the same expectation, not the same event."}
